lessons.push({
  title: "try, except, else and finally",
  minutes: 45,
  learn: ["Catch exceptions with try and except", "Handle several exception types in the right order", "Use else and finally correctly", "Know what not to catch"],
  why: "Real input is unreliable: users type letters instead of numbers, files go missing, networks fail. Handling errors makes a program robust instead of crashing.",
  realWorld: "A form asks for an age; if the user types &quot;abc&quot;, the program asks again instead of crashing. A download closes its file even if the network drops.",
  simple: "Put risky code in <code>try</code>. If an exception happens, Python jumps to the matching <code>except</code> block. <code>else</code> runs when nothing went wrong, and <code>finally</code> runs <em>always</em>, for cleanup.",
  detail: `<h3>Shape</h3>
<pre style="background:#16181c;color:#d8dee9;padding:12px 14px;border-radius:8px"><code>try:\n    risky code\nexcept ValueError:\n    handle bad value\nexcept (TypeError, KeyError) as error:\n    handle either, using the error object\nelse:\n    runs only if no exception occurred\nfinally:\n    always runs: cleanup</code></pre>
<ul><li><strong>Order matters</strong>: the first matching <code>except</code> wins, so put specific exceptions before general ones (<code>ValueError</code> before <code>Exception</code>).</li><li><strong>Keep the try block small</strong>: only the lines that can fail, so you do not hide unrelated bugs.</li><li><strong>Never use a bare <code>except:</code></strong>: it also catches <code>KeyboardInterrupt</code> and hides bugs. At most use <code>except Exception</code> at the top level of a program to log and exit cleanly.</li><li><strong>else</strong> keeps the &quot;success&quot; code out of the try block; <strong>finally</strong> runs even after <code>return</code>, <code>break</code> or an uncaught exception.</li></ul>
<h3>EAFP and LBYL</h3>
<p>Python style prefers <em>Easier to Ask Forgiveness than Permission</em>: try the operation and handle failure, rather than checking everything first (<em>Look Before You Leap</em>). For converting input, <code>try: int(text)</code> is more reliable than testing with <code>isdigit()</code> (which misses negatives and spaces).</p>
<h3>Exception hierarchy (part)</h3>
<p><code>BaseException</code> &rarr; <code>Exception</code> &rarr; <code>ArithmeticError</code> (&rarr; <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>), <code>LookupError</code> (&rarr; <code>IndexError</code>, <code>KeyError</code>), <code>ValueError</code>, <code>TypeError</code>, <code>OSError</code> (&rarr; <code>FileNotFoundError</code>, <code>PermissionError</code>). Catching a parent class catches its children.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `try:\n    ...\nexcept SomeError as e:\n    ...\nelse:\n    ...\nfinally:\n    ...`, note: "Specific exceptions first." },
  diagram: graph("try / except / else / finally flow", "Flow: run the try block; if an exception occurs, run the matching except; if not, run else; finally always runs; then continue.", 640, 260,
    [{ id: "t", k: "proc", t: "try block", x: 110, y: 40, w: 130, h: 40 }, { id: "d", k: "dec", t: "exception?", x: 110, y: 130, w: 150, h: 56 }, { id: "e", k: "proc", t: "except block", x: 360, y: 130, w: 150, h: 40 }, { id: "l", k: "proc", t: "else block", x: 110, y: 220, w: 130, h: 40 }, { id: "f", k: "proc", t: "finally (always)", x: 420, y: 220, w: 170, h: 40 }],
    [{ from: "t", to: "d" }, { from: "d", to: "e", label: "yes" }, { from: "d", to: "l", label: "no" }, { from: "e", to: "f" }, { from: "l", to: "f" }]),
  example: { code: `def parse(text):\n    try:\n        number = int(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        print(repr(text), "is not a number")\n        return None\n    else:\n        print(repr(text), "parsed as", number)\n        return number\n    finally:\n        print("done with", repr(text))\n\nparse("42")\nparse("4x2")\n\ndef divide(a, b):\n    try:\n        return a / b\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        return "cannot divide by zero"\n    except TypeError as error:\n        return "bad types: " + str(error)\n\nprint(divide(10, 4), divide(1, 0), divide("a", 2))`, out: `'42' parsed as 42\ndone with '42'\n'4x2' is not a number\ndone with '4x2'\n2.5 cannot divide by zero bad types: unsupported operand type(s) for /: 'str' and 'int'` },
  lineByLine: [["except ValueError:", "Runs only if int(text) fails with ValueError."], ["return None inside except", "The function leaves, but <code>finally</code> still runs first."], ["else:", "Runs only when the try block finished without an exception."], ["finally:", "Always prints the cleanup line, after either path."], ["except TypeError as error", "Keeps the exception object so its message can be used."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "<code>parse(\"4x2\")</code>:", headers: ["Step", "What happens"], rows: [["int(\"4x2\")", "raises ValueError"], ["except ValueError", "prints '4x2' is not a number, returns None"], ["finally", "prints done with '4x2' before leaving"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Ask again until the input is valid, the classic input-validation loop. Inputs: abc, -5, 30.", code: `while True:\n    text = input()\n    try:\n        age = int(text)\n        if age < 0:\n            raise ValueError("age cannot be negative")\n    except ValueError as error:\n        print("Invalid:", error)\n    else:\n        break\nprint("Age:", age)`, out: `Invalid: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'abc'\nInvalid: age cannot be negative\nAge: 30`, stdin: "abc\n-5\n30" },
  mistakes: ["Bare <code>except:</code> or <code>except Exception: pass</code> that silently hides bugs.", "Putting too much code inside try.", "Catching a general exception before a specific one (the specific one never runs).", "Using exceptions for ordinary control flow where an <code>if</code> is clearer.", "Forgetting that <code>finally</code> runs even when you <code>return</code>."],
  practices: ["Catch the narrowest exception that makes sense.", "Log or report the error; do not swallow it.", "Use <code>finally</code> or a <code>with</code> statement for cleanup.", "Re-raise with a bare <code>raise</code> when you cannot handle it."],
  interview: ["What do else and finally do in try?", "Why is bare except bad?", "What is EAFP?", "In which order should excepts be written?"],
  practiceQs: ["Write safe_int(text, default=0).", "What prints: try: return 1 finally: print('x')?", "Which exception does open('x.txt') raise if the file is missing?", "Why would except Exception come last?"],
  revision: ["try / except / else / finally.", "Specific before general; small try blocks.", "finally always runs.", "Never bare except."],
  hinglish: "try mein risky code rakho, error aaye to except chalta hai, error na aaye to else, aur finally hamesha chalta hai. Pehle specific exception likho, phir general. Bare except kabhi mat likho.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("When does the else block of a try run?", ["Always", "Only when no exception occurred", "Only on exception", "Never"], 1, "else means success."),
    mcq("When does finally run?", ["Only if there is an exception", "Only if there is none", "Always", "Never"], 2, "It is for cleanup."),
    mcq("What is printed?\n\ntry:\n    print(1 / 0)\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(\"a\")\nexcept Exception:\n    print(\"b\")", ["a", "b", "a then b", "Error"], 0, "The first matching except wins.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("What is printed?\n\ndef f():\n    try:\n        return \"try\"\n    finally:\n        print(\"cleanup\")\nprint(f())", ["try", "cleanup then try", "cleanup", "Error"], 1, "finally runs before the function returns.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("What is wrong with except: pass?", ["It hides every error, including bugs", "It is a syntax error", "It is too slow", "Nothing"], 0, "Errors vanish silently."),
  ],
  tasks: [task("Sum the valid numbers", "Read a line of tokens. Add up the tokens that are valid integers and count the ones that are not. Print the sum and the count of invalid tokens separated by a space.", "tokens = input().split()\n# sum valid integers, count invalid tokens\n", `tokens = input().split()\ntotal = 0\nbad = 0\nfor t in tokens:\n    try:\n        total += int(t)\n    except ValueError:\n        bad += 1\nprint(total, bad)`, [["10 abc 20 x", "30 2"], ["1 2 3", "6 0"]], [["10 abc 20 x", "30 2"], ["1 2 3", "6 0"], ["a b c", "0 3"], ["-5 5 3.5", "0 1"], ["7", "7 0"]])],
});

