lessons.push({
  title: "raise, Custom Exceptions and assert",
  minutes: 40,
  learn: ["Raise exceptions to signal that something is wrong", "Define your own exception classes", "Re-raise and chain exceptions", "Use assert for internal checks"],
  why: "Functions should refuse bad input loudly instead of returning nonsense. Custom exceptions let callers react to specific problems of your program, such as <code>InsufficientFunds</code>.",
  realWorld: "A banking module raises <code>InsufficientFunds</code> when a withdrawal is too large; the web layer catches it and shows a friendly message.",
  simple: "<code>raise SomeError(&quot;message&quot;)</code> stops normal flow and signals a problem. To make a new kind of error, write a class that inherits from <code>Exception</code>.",
  detail: `<h3>raise</h3>
<ul><li><code>raise ValueError("amount must be positive")</code> raises a new exception.</li><li>A bare <code>raise</code> inside an <code>except</code> block re-raises the current one after you did something (logging).</li><li><code>raise NewError("...") from original</code> keeps the original as the cause in the traceback.</li></ul>
<h3>Custom exceptions</h3>
<pre style="background:#16181c;color:#d8dee9;padding:12px 14px;border-radius:8px"><code>class InsufficientFunds(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, needed):\n        super().__init__(f"need {needed} more")\n        self.needed = needed</code></pre>
<p>Inherit from <code>Exception</code> (not <code>BaseException</code>). Name them with the suffix <code>Error</code> or a clear noun phrase. Create a small base class for your project (<code>class AppError(Exception)</code>) and derive specific ones from it, so callers can catch all of them at once.</p>
<h3>Raise or return an error value?</h3>
<p>Raise for conditions that mean the function cannot do its job (invalid argument, failure). Return normally for expected outcomes (a search that finds nothing may return <code>None</code>).</p>
<h3>assert</h3>
<p><code>assert condition, "message"</code> raises <code>AssertionError</code> if the condition is false. Use it for internal sanity checks and in tests, never to validate user input: assertions can be turned off with <code>python -O</code>.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `class AppError(Exception):\n    pass\nif amount <= 0:\n    raise ValueError("amount must be positive")\nassert isinstance(x, int), "x must be int"`, note: "Raise early, handle late." },
  diagram: chain("Raising and handling a custom exception", "Left to right: function raises InsufficientFunds, the call stack unwinds to the caller, the except block handles it and prints a message.",
    [{ k: "proc", t: "withdraw()\nraise InsufficientFunds" }, { k: "proc", t: "stack unwinds\nto the caller" }, { k: "proc", t: "except\nInsufficientFunds" }, { k: "io", t: "friendly\nmessage" }], { w: 150, gap: 30, hgt: 62 }),
  example: { code: `class InsufficientFunds(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, needed):\n        super().__init__(f"need {needed} more")\n        self.needed = needed\n\ndef withdraw(balance, amount):\n    if amount <= 0:\n        raise ValueError("amount must be positive")\n    if amount > balance:\n        raise InsufficientFunds(amount - balance)\n    return balance - amount\n\nfor amount in (200, 800, -5):\n    try:\n        print("balance:", withdraw(500, amount))\n    except InsufficientFunds as error:\n        print("failed:", error, "| needed =", error.needed)\n    except ValueError as error:\n        print("invalid:", error)\n\ntry:\n    try:\n        int("x")\n    except ValueError as original:\n        raise RuntimeError("bad config") from original\nexcept RuntimeError as error:\n    print(error, "| caused by:", type(error.__cause__).__name__)\n\ntry:\n    assert 1 + 1 == 3, "arithmetic broke"\nexcept AssertionError as error:\n    print("assertion:", error)`, out: `balance: 300\nfailed: need 300 more | needed = 300\ninvalid: amount must be positive\nbad config | caused by: ValueError\nassertion: arithmetic broke` },
  lineByLine: [["class InsufficientFunds(Exception)", "A new exception type. The constructor stores the shortfall in <code>needed</code>."], ["raise InsufficientFunds(amount - balance)", "Signals the problem to the caller."], ["except InsufficientFunds as error", "Catches only that kind; <code>error.needed</code> gives extra data."], ["raise RuntimeError(...) from original", "Translates a low-level error and keeps the cause."], ["assert ..., \"message\"", "Raises AssertionError with the message when the condition is false."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "<code>withdraw(500, 800)</code>:", headers: ["Step", "Result"], rows: [["amount > 0", "True, continue"], ["amount > balance (800 > 500)", "True"], ["raise InsufficientFunds(300)", "function stops; the caller's except runs"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "A small hierarchy: one base class lets callers catch every error of the application at once.", code: `class ShopError(Exception):\n    pass\n\nclass OutOfStock(ShopError):\n    pass\n\nclass UnknownItem(ShopError):\n    pass\n\nstock = {"pen": 2}\ndef buy(item):\n    if item not in stock:\n        raise UnknownItem(item)\n    if stock[item] == 0:\n        raise OutOfStock(item)\n    stock[item] -= 1\n\nfor name in ["pen", "pen", "pen", "ink"]:\n    try:\n        buy(name)\n        print("bought", name)\n    except ShopError as error:\n        print(type(error).__name__, error)`, out: `bought pen\nbought pen\nOutOfStock pen\nUnknownItem ink` },
  mistakes: ["Raising <code>Exception(\"...\")</code> everywhere, so nobody can tell errors apart.", "Using assert to validate user input.", "Catching and re-raising without information.", "Inheriting from BaseException.", "Raising a string (<code>raise \"error\"</code> is a TypeError)."],
  practices: ["Use built-in exceptions when they fit (ValueError, TypeError).", "Write clear messages: what was wrong and what was expected.", "Create a base exception for your package.", "Keep custom exceptions in one module."],
  interview: ["How do you create a custom exception?", "What does raise ... from ... do?", "assert versus raise?", "When should a function raise instead of returning None?"],
  practiceQs: ["Write a function that raises ValueError when given a negative age.", "Create NegativeAmountError and use it.", "Why is a bare raise useful in except?", "Why can assert not guard user input?"],
  revision: ["raise X(\"msg\"); bare raise re-raises.", "class MyError(Exception).", "raise ... from cause.", "assert = developer check."],
  hinglish: "raise se hum khud exception uthate hain. Apna exception banane ke liye Exception se inherit karo. assert sirf developer ke internal check ke liye hai, user input check ke liye nahi.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("Which base class should custom exceptions normally inherit from?", ["BaseException", "Exception", "object only", "str"], 1, "Exception is the base of ordinary errors."),
    mcq("What does a bare raise in an except block do?", ["Raises a new error", "Re-raises the current exception", "Ignores the error", "Ends the program silently"], 1, "It propagates the same exception."),
    mcq("What is raised when assert 1 == 2 fails?", ["ValueError", "AssertionError", "SyntaxError", "TypeError"], 1, "assert raises AssertionError."),
    mcq("What does this print?\n\nclass E(Exception): pass\ntry:\n    raise E(\"boom\")\nexcept E as e:\n    print(e)", ["boom", "E", "Exception", "None"], 0, "str(exception) is its message.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("Why not use assert for user input?", ["It is slow", "It can be disabled with -O", "It prints too much", "It needs imports"], 1, "Assertions may be removed."),
  ],
  tasks: [task("Withdraw with a custom exception", "Read the balance and the amount (one per line). Define a custom exception InsufficientFunds and a withdraw function that raises it when the amount exceeds the balance. Print Balance: <new balance> on success, or Insufficient funds: need <n> more on failure.", "class InsufficientFunds(Exception):\n    pass\n\ndef withdraw(balance, amount):\n    pass\n\nbalance = int(input())\namount = int(input())\n# call withdraw and print the result\n", `class InsufficientFunds(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, needed):\n        super().__init__(needed)\n        self.needed = needed\n\ndef withdraw(balance, amount):\n    if amount > balance:\n        raise InsufficientFunds(amount - balance)\n    return balance - amount\n\nbalance = int(input())\namount = int(input())\ntry:\n    print("Balance:", withdraw(balance, amount))\nexcept InsufficientFunds as error:\n    print("Insufficient funds: need", error.needed, "more")`, [["500\n200", "Balance: 300"], ["500\n800", "Insufficient funds: need 300 more"]], [["500\n200", "Balance: 300"], ["500\n800", "Insufficient funds: need 300 more"], ["100\n100", "Balance: 0"], ["0\n1", "Insufficient funds: need 1 more"]])],
});

