lessons.push({
  title: "Files: open, Modes, Reading, Writing and the with Statement",
  minutes: 50,
  learn: ["Open files in the right mode", "Read whole files, lines and chunks", "Write and append text", "Use with so files always close", "Handle missing files and encodings"],
  why: "Programs must remember things after they stop: scores, settings, logs, reports. Files are the simplest storage, and the same skills apply to reading input in coding tests.",
  realWorld: "Saving a contact book to disk, reading a log file line by line, exporting a report.",
  simple: "<code>open(name, mode)</code> gives you a file object. You read or write through it and then <strong>close</strong> it. The <code>with</code> statement closes it for you automatically, even after an error.",
  detail: `${table(["Mode", "Meaning", "If the file does not exist", "If it exists"], [["<code>\"r\"</code>", "read (default)", "FileNotFoundError", "read from the start"], ["<code>\"w\"</code>", "write", "created", "<strong>emptied</strong> first"], ["<code>\"a\"</code>", "append", "created", "write at the end"], ["<code>\"x\"</code>", "create exclusively", "created", "FileExistsError"], ["<code>\"r+\"</code>", "read and write", "FileNotFoundError", "keeps content"], ["add <code>b</code>", "binary (images, PDFs)", "-", "-"]])}
<h3>Always use with and an encoding</h3>
<pre style="background:#16181c;color:#d8dee9;padding:12px 14px;border-radius:8px"><code>with open("notes.txt", "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:\n    f.write("first line\\n")\n\nwith open("notes.txt", encoding="utf-8") as f:\n    for line in f:\n        print(line.rstrip("\\n"))</code></pre>
<p>Give <code>encoding="utf-8"</code> explicitly for text, so Hindi, emoji and symbols work the same on Windows, Linux and macOS.</p>
<h3>Reading</h3>
<ul><li><code>f.read()</code> the whole file as one string (small files only).</li><li><code>for line in f:</code> one line at a time: memory-friendly and the preferred way.</li><li><code>f.readline()</code> one line; <code>f.readlines()</code> a list of all lines.</li></ul>
<p>Each line keeps its newline character; remove it with <code>line.rstrip("\\n")</code> or <code>line.strip()</code>.</p>
<h3>Writing</h3>
<p><code>f.write(text)</code> writes exactly the text, with no newline added; <code>f.writelines(list_of_lines)</code> does not add newlines either. <code>print(text, file=f)</code> adds one.</p>
<h3>Context managers</h3>
<p><code>with</code> uses an object's <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code> methods; the file's <code>__exit__</code> closes it. You can write your own (Module 11). A file that is not closed may lose buffered data.</p>
<h3>Reading standard input</h3>
<p>Coding platforms give input through <code>stdin</code>. <code>for line in sys.stdin:</code> or <code>sys.stdin.read()</code> read it like a file, which is handy when the number of lines is unknown.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as f:\n    text = f.read()\nwith open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:\n    f.write("more\\n")\nfor line in sys.stdin: ...`, note: "w erases an existing file." },
  diagram: chain("Life of a file object", "Left to right: open the file, read or write inside the with block, the with statement closes it automatically even on errors.",
    [{ k: "proc", t: "open(path, mode)" }, { k: "proc", t: "read / write\ninside with" }, { k: "proc", t: "automatic\nclose" }, { k: "term", t: "data safely\non disk" }], { w: 145, gap: 30, hgt: 56 }),
  example: { code: `import tempfile, pathlib\nwith tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as folder:\n    path = pathlib.Path(folder) / "notes.txt"\n    with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:\n        f.write("first line\\n")\n        f.write("second line\\n")\n    with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:\n        print("third line", file=f)\n    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:\n        for number, line in enumerate(f, start=1):\n            print(number, line.rstrip("\\n"))\n    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:\n        print(len(f.read()))\n    try:\n        open(pathlib.Path(folder) / "missing.txt")\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        print("no such file")`, out: `1 first line\n2 second line\n3 third line\n34\nno such file` },
  lineByLine: [["open(path, \"w\", ...)", "Creates the file (or empties it) and allows writing."], ["f.write(\"first line\\n\")", "write adds no newline, so <code>\\n</code> is written explicitly."], ["open(path, \"a\")", "Opens for appending at the end."], ["print(\"third line\", file=f)", "print can write to a file and adds the newline."], ["enumerate(f, start=1)", "Numbers the lines as they are read."], ["len(f.read())", "Number of characters including the three newline characters: 11 + 12 + 11 = 34."], ["except FileNotFoundError", "The file was never created, so the error is caught."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "File contents after each step:", headers: ["After", "File contains"], rows: [["mode w, write twice", "first line, second line (2 lines)"], ["mode a, print", "+ third line (3 lines)"], ["mode r", "unchanged (reading)"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Count lines, words and characters of text arriving on standard input, like the wc tool.", code: `import sys\nlines = words = chars = 0\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n    line = line.rstrip("\\n")\n    lines += 1\n    words += len(line.split())\n    chars += len(line)\nprint(lines, words, chars)`, out: `2 3 14`, stdin: "hello world\nfoo" },
  mistakes: ["Opening with <code>\"w\"</code> when you meant <code>\"a\"</code> and erasing the data.", "Forgetting <code>close()</code> or not using <code>with</code>.", "Forgetting newlines when writing.", "Reading a huge file with <code>read()</code>.", "Leaving out the encoding on Windows and getting garbled text."],
  practices: ["Always use <code>with</code> and an explicit encoding.", "Loop over the file object for large files.", "Handle <code>FileNotFoundError</code> and <code>PermissionError</code>.", "Use <code>pathlib</code> for paths (next lessons)."],
  interview: ["What does the with statement do?", "Difference between modes w and a?", "How do you read a large file efficiently?", "Why specify the encoding?"],
  practiceQs: ["Write the numbers 1 to 5 to a file, one per line, then read and sum them.", "Append a line to an existing log file.", "What happens if you open a missing file with mode \"r\"? With \"w\"?", "Why does f.write(5) fail?"],
  revision: ["Modes r, w, a, x, b.", "with closes the file.", "for line in f for big files.", "write adds no newline."],
  hinglish: "open(naam, mode) se file khulti hai. r padhne ke liye, w likhne ke liye (purana data mit jata hai), a end mein jodne ke liye. with ka istemal karo taaki file apne aap band ho. Encoding utf-8 zaroor likho.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("Which mode erases an existing file's content when opened?", ["r", "a", "w", "r+"], 2, "w truncates the file."),
    mcq("Which statement closes the file automatically?", ["try", "with", "while", "import"], 1, "The with block calls close on exit."),
    mcq("What does f.write(\"hi\") add at the end?", ["A newline", "Nothing extra", "A space", "A tab"], 1, "write adds exactly the text.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("What happens for open(\"nofile.txt\") in mode r when the file is missing?", ["It is created", "FileNotFoundError", "Returns None", "Empty string"], 1, "Reading needs an existing file."),
    mcq("What is the best way to process a very large text file?", ["f.read() then split", "for line in f", "f.readlines()", "Open it twice"], 1, "Iterating reads one line at a time."),
  ],
  tasks: [task("Line, word and character count", "Read all lines from standard input (use sys.stdin). Print the number of lines, the number of words and the number of characters (not counting the newline characters), separated by spaces.", "import sys\n# read every line from sys.stdin and count\n", `import sys\nlines = words = chars = 0\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n    line = line.rstrip("\\n")\n    lines += 1\n    words += len(line.split())\n    chars += len(line)\nprint(lines, words, chars)`, [["hello world\nfoo", "2 3 14"], ["a b c", "1 3 5"]], [["hello world\nfoo", "2 3 14"], ["a b c", "1 3 5"], ["one", "1 1 3"], ["x y\nz\n w v u", "3 6 10"]])],
});

