lessons.push({
  title: "CSV and JSON Files",
  minutes: 45,
  learn: ["Read and write CSV with the csv module", "Convert Python data to and from JSON", "Know how Python types map to JSON", "Handle malformed data"],
  why: "CSV (spreadsheets) and JSON (web APIs and settings) are the two most common data formats you will meet. Both have standard modules, so you never need to split text by hand.",
  realWorld: "Importing a class list from Excel (CSV), saving app settings as JSON, reading the response of a weather API.",
  simple: "<strong>CSV</strong> is rows of values separated by commas: a plain-text table. <strong>JSON</strong> is nested data written with braces, brackets, strings, numbers, true/false and null. Python converts both to normal lists and dictionaries.",
  detail: `<h3>csv</h3>
<ul><li><code>csv.reader(f)</code> gives each row as a list of strings; <code>csv.DictReader(f)</code> uses the header row and gives dictionaries.</li><li><code>csv.writer(f).writerow([...])</code> and <code>csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=[...])</code> write rows. Open files with <code>newline=""</code> so the module controls line endings.</li><li>Everything read from CSV is a <strong>string</strong>: convert numbers yourself (<code>int(row["mark"])</code>).</li><li>Fields that contain commas or quotes are quoted automatically; never build CSV lines with <code>",".join</code>.</li></ul>
<h3>json</h3>
${table(["Function", "Purpose"], [["<code>json.dumps(obj)</code>", "Python object &rarr; JSON text (string)"], ["<code>json.loads(text)</code>", "JSON text &rarr; Python object"], ["<code>json.dump(obj, f)</code>", "write JSON to a file"], ["<code>json.load(f)</code>", "read JSON from a file"]])}
${table(["JSON", "Python"], [["object <code>{}</code>", "dict"], ["array <code>[]</code>", "list (a tuple becomes an array too)"], ["string, number", "str, int or float"], ["true / false", "True / False"], ["null", "None"]])}
<p>Useful options: <code>indent=2</code> for readable output, <code>sort_keys=True</code> for stable order, <code>ensure_ascii=False</code> to keep non-English text readable. JSON keys are always strings (a dictionary key 1 becomes "1"). Invalid JSON raises <code>json.JSONDecodeError</code> (a subclass of ValueError). Never load untrusted data with <code>eval</code>.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `import csv, json\nwith open(path, newline="", encoding="utf-8") as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f): ...\ndata = json.loads(text)\ntext = json.dumps(data, indent=2)`, note: "CSV values are strings." },
  diagram: graph("Data formats and Python types", "A CSV file becomes a list of dictionaries through DictReader, a JSON file becomes dictionaries and lists through json.load, and both can be written back.", 640, 190,
    [{ id: "c", k: "io", t: "marks.csv", x: 90, y: 50, w: 130, h: 40 }, { id: "j", k: "io", t: "data.json", x: 90, y: 140, w: 130, h: 40 }, { id: "p", k: "proc", t: "Python lists\nand dicts", x: 340, y: 95, w: 160, h: 60 }, { id: "o", k: "io", t: "report / API\nresponse", x: 560, y: 95, w: 150, h: 56 }],
    [{ from: "c", to: "p", label: "DictReader" }, { from: "j", to: "p", label: "json.load" }, { from: "p", to: "o", label: "dump / writer" }]),
  example: { code: `import csv, io, json\ntext = "name,mark\\nAsha,80\\nRavi,92\\n"\nrows = list(csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(text)))\nprint(rows)\nprint(sum(int(r["mark"]) for r in rows))\n\nout = io.StringIO()\nwriter = csv.writer(out, lineterminator="\\n")\nwriter.writerow(["name", "note"])\nwriter.writerow(["Zoya", "likes, commas"])\nprint(out.getvalue().strip())\n\nrecord = {"name": "Asha", "marks": [80, 90], "active": True, "address": None}\nencoded = json.dumps(record)\nprint(encoded)\nprint(json.loads(encoded) == record)\nprint(json.dumps({"b": 1, "a": [1, 2]}, indent=2, sort_keys=True))\ntry:\n    json.loads("{bad json}")\nexcept json.JSONDecodeError:\n    print("invalid JSON")`, out: `[{'name': 'Asha', 'mark': '80'}, {'name': 'Ravi', 'mark': '92'}]\n172\nname,note\nZoya,"likes, commas"\n{"name": "Asha", "marks": [80, 90], "active": true, "address": null}\nTrue\n{\n  "a": [\n    1,\n    2\n  ],\n  "b": 1\n}\ninvalid JSON` },
  lineByLine: [["io.StringIO(text)", "Makes a string behave like a file: the same code works for real files."], ["csv.DictReader", "Each row becomes a dictionary using the header."], ["int(r[\"mark\"])", "CSV gives strings; convert before adding."], ["writer.writerow([\"Zoya\", \"likes, commas\"])", "The field containing a comma is quoted automatically."], ["json.dumps(record)", "True becomes true, None becomes null."], ["indent=2, sort_keys=True", "Readable, stable output."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "How DictReader builds the first row:", headers: ["Header", "Line", "Dictionary"], rows: [["name,mark", "Asha,80", "{'name': 'Asha', 'mark': '80'}"], ["(same header)", "Ravi,92", "{'name': 'Ravi', 'mark': '92'}"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Round trip: save settings to JSON text and read them back, including a tuple and a non-string key that change type.", code: `import json\nsettings = {"theme": "dark", "size": (10, 20), 1: "one"}\ntext = json.dumps(settings)\nprint(text)\nback = json.loads(text)\nprint(back["size"], list(back))`, out: `{"theme": "dark", "size": [10, 20], "1": "one"}\n[10, 20] ['theme', 'size', '1']` },
  mistakes: ["Forgetting <code>newline=\"\"</code> when opening CSV files (blank rows on Windows).", "Forgetting that CSV numbers are strings.", "Building CSV by joining with commas.", "Expecting tuples or integer keys to survive a JSON round trip.", "Using <code>eval</code> to parse data."],
  practices: ["Use <code>DictReader</code>/<code>DictWriter</code> for readable code.", "Validate and convert fields immediately after reading.", "Catch <code>json.JSONDecodeError</code> for external data.", "Use <code>indent=2</code> for files people will read."],
  interview: ["Difference between json.load and json.loads?", "How does Python map JSON types?", "Why use the csv module instead of split(\",\")?", "What happens to a tuple in JSON?"],
  practiceQs: ["Convert [{\"a\": 1}] to JSON text and back.", "Write rows with a comma inside a value; how does csv store it?", "How do you pretty-print JSON?", "What does json.dumps(None) return?"],
  revision: ["csv.reader/DictReader/writer/DictWriter.", "json.dumps/loads (text), dump/load (files).", "CSV = strings; JSON maps to dict/list/None/bool.", "Handle JSONDecodeError."],
  hinglish: "CSV mein comma se alag rows hoti hain aur sab kuch string hota hai. JSON mein dict, list, true/false aur null hote hain. csv aur json modules use karo, khud split(',') mat karo.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What type are the values read by csv.reader?", ["int", "str", "float", "Depends on the data"], 1, "Everything is a string.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("What does json.dumps(True) return?", ["True", "true", "'True'", "1"], 1, "JSON booleans are lowercase.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("Which function reads JSON from a file object?", ["json.loads", "json.load", "json.read", "json.parse"], 1, "load works on files; loads works on strings."),
    mcq("What does a tuple become after a JSON round trip?", ["tuple", "list", "set", "string"], 1, "JSON has only arrays.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("Why open CSV files with newline=\"\"?", ["Speed", "So the csv module handles line endings itself", "To allow Unicode", "It is optional decoration"], 1, "Otherwise extra blank lines can appear on Windows."),
  ],
  tasks: [task("Best student from CSV text", "Standard input holds CSV text with the header name,mark followed by rows. Using csv.DictReader on sys.stdin, print the name of the student with the highest mark (the first one if there is a tie).", "import csv, sys\n# read the CSV from sys.stdin and print the top student\n", `import csv, sys\nbest = None\nfor row in csv.DictReader(sys.stdin):\n    if best is None or int(row["mark"]) > int(best["mark"]):\n        best = row\nprint(best["name"])`, [["name,mark\nAsha,80\nRavi,92", "Ravi"], ["name,mark\nA,5\nB,5", "A"]], [["name,mark\nAsha,80\nRavi,92", "Ravi"], ["name,mark\nA,5\nB,5", "A"], ["name,mark\nKiran,0", "Kiran"], ["name,mark\nx,10\ny,30\nz,20", "y"]])],
});

